---MODULE---
@moduledoc """
Finds the most frequent element in a list of elements.

If multiple elements have the same highest frequency, the one that appears
first in the list is returned.

The function runs in linear time relative to the length of the list.
"""

defmodule Solution do
  @doc """
  Returns a tuple `{element, frequency}` of the most frequent element in `list`.
  In case of a tie, the element that appears first in the list is returned.

  ## Examples

      iex> Solution.find_most_frequent_elements([1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 4, 4, 4])
      {4, 4}

      iex> Solution.find_most_frequent_elements([8, 8, 8, 8, 8, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9])
      {8, 5}

      iex> Solution.find_most_frequent_elements([2, 3, 1, 3, 2, 4, 6, 7, 9, 2, 19])
      {2, 3}
  """
  @spec find_most_frequent_elements(list()) :: {any(), non_neg_integer()}
  def find_most_frequent_elements(list) when is_list(list) and length(list) > 0 do
    # First pass: count frequencies
    frequencies = Enum.reduce(list, %{}, fn element, acc ->
      Map.update(acc, element, 1, &(&1 + 1))
    end)

    # To handle ties correctly (return the element that appears first),
    # we need to iterate through the list in order and track the best candidate.
    # We'll use reduce to find the max frequency element, keeping the first one
    # encountered with that max frequency.

    # First, find the maximum frequency value
    max_frequency = frequencies |> Map.values() |> Enum.max()

    # Then, find the first element in the original list that has this frequency
    result = list |> Enum.find(fn element ->
      frequencies[element] == max_frequency
    end)

    {result, max_frequency}
  end
end
---TEST---
